PNT.registerModule({"id": "m02", "number": 2, "title": "How GNSS Works", "subtitle": "", "version": "0.1", "objectives": ["Describe the basic GNSS system segments and the role of the user receiver.", "Explain why GNSS satellites broadcast both a ranging signal and navigation data.", "Explain, conceptually, how a receiver acquires and tracks satellite signals.", "Define pseudorange and explain why it is not the same as true geometric range.", "Explain the role of satellite clocks, receiver clock bias and time synchronisation.", "Distinguish ephemeris from almanac data.", "Explain how measurements from multiple satellites are combined to solve for Position, Velocity and Time.", "Recognise the importance of satellite geometry and coordinate/reference systems without needing to perform the mathematics.", "Explain why a coordinate is only meaningful when its reference frame, datum and height reference are understood.", "Describe the main processing chain inside a GNSS receiver at a system-engineering level."], "lessons": [{"id": "from-pnt-capability-to-gnss-engineering", "title": "From PNT Capability to GNSS Engineering", "html": "<p>Module 1 established that PNT is a capability and GNSS is one family of technologies used to provide it. This module moves inside a GNSS system. The objective is not to turn the learner into a receiver designer or orbital mechanics specialist. It is to build a technically credible mental model of how signals transmitted from moving satellites become a Position, Velocity and Time (PVT) solution inside a receiver.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">The question this module answers</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A GNSS receiver has no direct way to 'see' its own latitude and longitude. How does it use radio signals, time and satellite orbit information to calculate where it is and what time it is?</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m02-learning-objectives\">Learning objectives</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Describe the basic GNSS system segments and the role of the user receiver.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why GNSS satellites broadcast both a ranging signal and navigation data.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain, conceptually, how a receiver acquires and tracks satellite signals.</p></li>\n<li><p>Define pseudorange and explain why it is not the same as true geometric range.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain the role of satellite clocks, receiver clock bias and time synchronisation.</p></li>\n<li><p>Distinguish ephemeris from almanac data.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain how measurements from multiple satellites are combined to solve for Position, Velocity and Time.</p></li>\n<li><p>Recognise the importance of satellite geometry and coordinate/reference systems without needing to perform the mathematics.</p></li>\n<li><p>Explain why a coordinate is only meaningful when its reference frame, datum and height reference are understood.</p></li>\n<li><p>Describe the main processing chain inside a GNSS receiver at a system-engineering level.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"m02-what-we-are-deliberately-not-covering-yet\">What we are deliberately not covering yet</h2>\n<p>GNSS measurement errors, multipath, ionospheric and tropospheric effects, Dilution of Precision (DOP), augmentation, RTK, PPP and detailed performance engineering are developed in Modules 3 and 4. Threats such as jamming and spoofing are introduced later. Here we first establish how the nominal system works.</p>"}, {"id": "the-gnss-system", "title": "The GNSS System", "html": "<p>A GNSS is more than satellites. At a high level it can be considered as three interacting segments: the space segment, the control segment and the user segment.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Segment</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Purpose</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Typical elements</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Space segment</td>\n<td>Transmits ranging signals and navigation information from orbit.</td>\n<td>GNSS satellites, onboard clocks, antennas and payloads.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Control segment</td>\n<td>Monitors and manages the constellation and maintains information needed by the satellites.</td>\n<td>Ground monitoring stations, control facilities, orbit/clock determination and upload functions.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>User segment</td>\n<td>Receives and processes the signals to produce PNT information.</td>\n<td>Antennas, RF front ends, GNSS receivers, navigation processors and applications.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: The GNSS System\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m02-image1.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m02-satellites-are-moving-reference-points\">2.1 Satellites are moving reference points</h2>\n<p>GNSS positioning depends on knowing where the transmitting satellites were when their signals were sent. The satellites therefore act as moving reference points. A receiver must obtain sufficiently accurate satellite orbit information and measure its apparent distance from several satellites.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Key mental model</p><div class=\"callout-body\">GNSS positioning requires two things from each useful satellite: an estimate of where the satellite was, and a measurement related to how far away it was. The navigation message helps provide the first; signal timing provides the second.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m02-european-gnss-context-galileo-and-egnos\">2.2 European GNSS context: Galileo and EGNOS</h2>\n<p>GNSS is a family of systems rather than a single global service. In a European context, engineers will commonly encounter Galileo, the European Union's global navigation satellite system, alongside GPS and other constellations. Galileo provides multiple signals and services designed for different user needs.</p>\n<p>Engineers may also encounter EGNOS, the European Geostationary Navigation Overlay Service. EGNOS is an augmentation system rather than a separate GNSS constellation: it uses monitoring infrastructure and geostationary broadcasting to provide corrections and integrity-related information for supported GNSS services. Augmentation is developed properly in Module 4.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m02-engineering-point\">Engineering point</h3>\n<p>Do not treat every satellite or GNSS service as interchangeable. When a requirement depends on a particular constellation, signal, service or augmentation capability, identify that dependency explicitly rather than writing only “GNSS”.</p>"}, {"id": "what-does-a-gnss-satellite-broadcast", "title": "What Does a GNSS Satellite Broadcast?", "html": "<p>The radio signal received by a GNSS receiver carries more than a simple timestamp. At a conceptual level, the receiver uses identifiable ranging codes together with navigation data. Different constellations and services implement these in different ways, but the underlying engineering purpose is similar.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Information / signal element</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Why the receiver needs it</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ranging code</td>\n<td>Allows the receiver to identify and track a satellite signal and estimate signal travel time.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Satellite clock information</td>\n<td>Allows correction for predicted satellite clock offset.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ephemeris</td>\n<td>Provides precise orbit information for the transmitting satellite over its period of validity.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Almanac</td>\n<td>Provides lower-precision orbit/status information covering the wider constellation and can assist satellite acquisition.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Health / service information</td>\n<td>Helps indicate whether particular satellite information or signals should be used.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Time-system / correction data</td>\n<td>Supports interpretation of GNSS system time and, depending on the service, relevant corrections.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: What Does a GNSS Satellite Broadcast?\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m02-image2.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "finding-a-satellite-acquisition-and-tracking", "title": "Finding a Satellite: Acquisition and Tracking", "html": "<p>A receiver cannot use a satellite merely because it is above the horizon. It first has to detect a usable signal and identify which satellite produced it. GNSS signals use known code structures that allow the receiver to search for and correlate the received signal with locally generated replicas.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m02-acquisition\">4.1 Acquisition</h2>\n<p>Acquisition is the initial search process. The receiver searches across possible code timing and frequency offset until it finds a correlation consistent with a particular satellite signal. Frequency search is necessary partly because the satellite and receiver are moving relative to one another, creating Doppler shift.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m02-tracking\">4.2 Tracking</h2>\n<p>Once acquired, the receiver continuously tracks the signal. Tracking keeps the locally generated code and carrier aligned with the received signal so that measurements and navigation data can continue to be extracted as conditions change.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Finding a Satellite: Acquisition and Tracking\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m02-image3.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Why this matters operationally</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A receiver that cannot acquire or maintain tracking cannot simply ‘use the satellite’. Later modules will show how signal power, obstruction, interference, dynamics and multipath can affect this process.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "from-signal-travel-time-to-pseudorange", "title": "From Signal Travel Time to Pseudorange", "html": "<p>The fundamental GNSS code measurement is based on the time taken for a signal to travel from the satellite antenna to the receiver antenna. Multiplying this measured travel time by the speed of light produces an apparent range.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Pseudorange ≈ measured signal travel time × speed of light</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>It is called a pseudorange because the raw measurement is not the exact geometric distance between satellite and receiver. The measurement includes effects such as the difference between satellite and receiver clocks and, in real systems, propagation and equipment effects that will be examined in Module 3.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: From Signal Travel Time to Pseudorange\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m02-image4.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Important terminology</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Range is the geometric distance. Pseudorange is the receiver's apparent range measurement derived from signal timing. Treating the two as identical hides the corrections and errors that GNSS processing must manage.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "clocks-the-hidden-core-of-gnss", "title": "Clocks: The Hidden Core of GNSS", "html": "<p>GNSS works because time is measured extremely precisely. Satellites carry highly stable clocks and broadcast information that allows their clock behaviour to be accounted for. The receiver also has a clock, but a typical user receiver does not carry an atomic clock of equivalent quality.</p>\n<p>The receiver clock is therefore not assumed to be perfectly synchronised with GNSS system time. Its clock bias is treated as an additional unknown in the navigation solution.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Unknowns: X position + Y position + Z position + receiver clock bias</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>This gives the familiar minimum of four independent satellite measurements for a conventional three-dimensional solution. With four suitable pseudorange measurements, the receiver can solve simultaneously for three spatial coordinates and its clock offset. Real receivers commonly use more than four satellites and estimate a solution using an overdetermined set of measurements.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Clocks: The Hidden Core of GNSS\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m02-image5.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "ephemeris-and-almanac", "title": "Ephemeris and Almanac", "html": "<p>These terms frequently appear in GNSS discussions and should become automatic. Both describe satellite orbit information, but they are used at different levels of precision and scope.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Data</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Scope</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Precision / purpose</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ephemeris</td>\n<td>Detailed information for the individual transmitting satellite.</td>\n<td>Used to calculate that satellite's position accurately enough for the navigation solution during the data's validity period.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Almanac</td>\n<td>Coarser information covering satellites across the constellation.</td>\n<td>Helps the receiver understand broadly where satellites should be and can support more efficient acquisition and constellation awareness.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-principle\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Easy way to remember it</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Ephemeris: precise information for the satellite you are measuring. Almanac: a coarser 'directory' of the wider constellation.</div></aside>\n<p>The distinction matters because knowing that a satellite exists is not enough. To turn a range measurement into a position solution, the receiver needs sufficiently accurate knowledge of the satellite's location at the relevant transmission time.</p>"}, {"id": "where-is-the-satellite", "title": "Where is the Satellite?", "html": "<p>Satellites orbit the Earth while the Earth itself rotates. GNSS calculations therefore require defined coordinate and time reference systems. Engineers commonly encounter Earth-Centred, Earth-Fixed (ECEF) Cartesian coordinates and geodetic latitude, longitude and height.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m02-earth-centred-earth-fixed\">8.1 Earth-Centred, Earth-Fixed</h2>\n<p>ECEF uses X, Y and Z axes with an origin associated with the Earth's centre of mass and rotates with the Earth. It is useful for the underlying geometry of satellite and receiver positions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m02-latitude-longitude-and-height\">8.2 Latitude, longitude and height</h2>\n<p>Applications commonly want a human-meaningful geodetic position rather than raw X, Y and Z coordinates. The computed position can therefore be expressed relative to an appropriate geodetic reference frame, for example WGS 84 in GPS contexts.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Where is the Satellite?\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m02-image6.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warning\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Engineering caution</p><div class=\"callout-body\">A coordinate is incomplete without its reference frame and, where relevant, height reference. '51° N, 2° W' looks precise but engineering-grade positioning requires clarity about the datum/reference system.</div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"m02-reference-frames-datums-and-height\">8.3 Reference frames, datums and height</h2>\n<p>A coordinate is not a complete position description by itself. It must be interpreted within a defined reference system. The same physical location can be represented by different numerical coordinates when different reference frames, datums, transformations or height references are used.</p>\n<p>At this level, three ideas are sufficient:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><p>Reference frame: defines the coordinate framework used to describe positions consistently relative to the Earth.</p></li>\n<li><p>Datum/reference system: provides the practical geodetic definition used to realise that framework. WGS 84 is commonly encountered in GPS and geospatial applications.</p></li>\n<li><p>Height reference: establishes what “height” is measured relative to. Ellipsoidal height from GNSS is not automatically the same as an orthometric or mean-sea-level-style height used by a map, survey or application.</p></li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"m02-why-this-matters-in-integrated-systems\">Why this matters in integrated systems</h3>\n<p>Two systems can each produce internally accurate positions and still disagree if they use different reference frames, datums, transformations or height conventions. This becomes particularly important when GNSS is integrated with maps, survey data, inertial systems, terrain databases, targeting data or other external geospatial sources.</p>\n<p>A useful engineering chain is:</p>\n<p><strong>measurement → reference system → coordinate representation → transformation (if required) → application</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"m02-engineering-caution\">Engineering caution</h3>\n<p>Never assume that matching latitude, longitude and height field names mean two systems are using compatible spatial references. Interface requirements should define the reference frame/datum, coordinate representation, height reference and any required transformation.</p>"}, {"id": "solving-for-position-velocity-and-time", "title": "Solving for Position, Velocity and Time", "html": "<p>Once the receiver is tracking enough satellites, has decoded the required navigation data and formed usable measurements, it can estimate its navigation solution. The exact algorithms can become mathematically sophisticated, but the system-level flow is straightforward.</p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Acquire and track signals from satellites in view.</p>\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Identify the satellites and decode the navigation data needed for the solution.</p>\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Determine satellite positions and account for satellite clock information.</p>\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Measure pseudoranges from signal timing.</p>\n<p><strong>5.</strong> Apply relevant measurement models and corrections.</p>\n<p><strong>6.</strong> Combine measurements from multiple satellites and solve for receiver position and clock bias.</p>\n<p><strong>7.</strong> Estimate velocity, commonly using changes in measurements and/or Doppler-related observables.</p>\n<p><strong>8.</strong> Output Position, Velocity and Time (PVT) to the application.</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Solving for Position, Velocity and Time\" height=\"800\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m02-image7.webp\" width=\"1600\"/></button></figure>\n<h2 id=\"m02-more-satellites-do-not-simply-mean-more-accuracy\">9.1 More satellites do not simply mean 'more accuracy'</h2>\n<p>Using additional valid measurements can improve robustness and estimation, but the quality of a GNSS solution also depends on satellite geometry and measurement quality. Four satellites clustered in an unfavourable part of the sky do not provide the same geometry as well-distributed satellites. Module 3 develops this through Dilution of Precision (DOP).</p>\n<figure class=\"figure\"><button aria-label=\"Enlarge diagram\" class=\"figure-zoom\" type=\"button\"><img alt=\"Diagram: Solving for Position, Velocity and Time\" height=\"1024\" loading=\"lazy\" src=\"assets/img/m02-image8.webp\" width=\"1536\"/></button></figure>"}, {"id": "a-receiver-is-a-processing-system", "title": "A Receiver is a Processing System", "html": "<p>It is useful to stop thinking of a GNSS receiver as a black box that outputs coordinates. It is a signal-processing and estimation system with dependencies at each stage.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Stage</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Question an engineer might ask</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Antenna / RF</td>\n<td>Can the required signals reach the receiver at sufficient quality?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Acquisition</td>\n<td>Can the receiver find and identify the required signals?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Tracking</td>\n<td>Can it maintain lock as the environment and dynamics change?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Data decoding</td>\n<td>Can it obtain valid clock, orbit and service information?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Measurement formation</td>\n<td>Are the pseudorange and other observables usable?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Navigation solution</td>\n<td>Are enough suitable measurements available and is the geometry adequate?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PVT output / integration</td>\n<td>How is the solution represented, timestamped, monitored and consumed by the application?</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Why this mental model becomes important later</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Jamming, spoofing, multipath, bad satellite data, antenna faults, receiver faults and integration errors can affect different parts of this chain. Resilient PNT engineering requires knowing where a failure can enter and how it propagates.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "worked-conceptual-example", "title": "Worked Conceptual Example", "html": "<p>Imagine a vehicle starts its GNSS receiver in an open area. The receiver searches for known satellite signals and acquires several of them. It tracks their ranging codes and decodes navigation data. From the ephemeris it calculates where each satellite was at transmission time. From signal timing it forms pseudorange measurements.</p>\n<p>Because its own clock is imperfect, all of those apparent ranges contain a common receiver-clock component. The navigation solver uses measurements from at least four suitably placed satellites to estimate X, Y, Z and that clock bias together. The result is transformed into the position representation required by the application, while the receiver also provides velocity and time.</p>\n<p>Nothing in that process required the satellite to know where the vehicle was. In normal GNSS operation the satellites broadcast; the receiver listens and calculates its own solution.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">Useful conversation point</p><div class=\"callout-body\">Standard GNSS positioning is fundamentally passive at the user end: receiving GNSS signals does not inherently require the receiver to transmit its position back to the satellites.</div></aside>"}, {"id": "module-summary", "title": "Module Summary", "html": "<ul>\n<li><p>A GNSS comprises space, control and user segments; the receiver is only one part of the overall system.</p></li>\n<li><p>Satellites provide identifiable ranging signals and navigation data needed to interpret those measurements.</p></li>\n<li><p>Receivers acquire signals, track them, decode navigation data and form observables before calculating PVT.</p></li>\n<li><p>Pseudorange is an apparent distance derived from signal travel time, not the exact geometric range.</p></li>\n<li><p>Satellite and receiver clocks are central to GNSS; receiver clock bias is estimated alongside three-dimensional position.</p></li>\n<li><p>Ephemeris provides precise satellite-specific orbit information; almanac provides coarser constellation-wide information.</p></li>\n<li><p>Coordinate and time reference systems are fundamental to expressing satellite and user states consistently.</p></li>\n<li><p>Reference frame, datum and height conventions must be explicit when GNSS outputs are integrated with other geospatial data or systems.</p></li>\n<li><p>At least four suitable measurements are normally needed to solve X, Y, Z and receiver clock bias, while real receivers often use many more.</p></li>\n<li><p>Satellite count alone does not determine solution quality; geometry and measurement quality also matter.</p></li>\n<li><p>Thinking of a GNSS receiver as a processing chain prepares us to understand errors, threats and resilience later in the course.</p></li>\n</ul>"}, {"id": "key-terms", "title": "Key Terms", "html": "<div class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\"><table>\n\n<thead>\n<tr class=\"header\">\n<th><strong>Term</strong></th>\n<th><strong>Working definition</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PVT</td>\n<td>Position, Velocity and Time: the core navigation solution commonly produced by a GNSS receiver.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Space segment</td>\n<td>The orbiting satellites and their PNT signal payloads.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Control segment</td>\n<td>Ground infrastructure that monitors and manages the GNSS constellation and supporting data.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>User segment</td>\n<td>Receivers and user equipment that consume GNSS signals.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Acquisition</td>\n<td>Initial search for and identification of a satellite signal.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Tracking</td>\n<td>Continuous alignment with an acquired signal so measurements and data can be extracted.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>PRN code</td>\n<td>A known pseudo-random code structure used to distinguish/range GNSS signals.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Pseudorange</td>\n<td>Apparent satellite-to-receiver range derived from signal travel time and containing clock and other effects.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Ephemeris</td>\n<td>Precise orbital parameters used to calculate the transmitting satellite's position.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Almanac</td>\n<td>Coarser constellation-wide satellite orbit/status information.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Receiver clock bias</td>\n<td>Difference between the receiver's clock and the relevant GNSS time reference, estimated in the solution.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>ECEF</td>\n<td>Earth-Centred, Earth-Fixed Cartesian coordinate system that rotates with the Earth.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>WGS 84</td>\n<td>A global reference system commonly encountered in GPS/geospatial positioning.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Doppler</td>\n<td>Observed frequency shift caused by relative motion, useful in acquisition/tracking and velocity estimation.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"odd\">\n<td>Reference frame</td>\n<td>Defined coordinate framework used to express positions consistently relative to a reference.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr class=\"even\">\n<td>Height reference</td>\n<td>The reference surface or datum relative to which height is expressed; GNSS ellipsoidal height is not automatically equivalent to orthometric height.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>"}, {"id": "further-learning", "title": "Further Learning", "html": "<p>Recommended open-source resources for learners who want to go deeper:</p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: GNSS Receivers General Introduction</strong><br/>\nA concise technical introduction to receiver processing, pseudorange and PVT.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Receivers_General_Introduction\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Receivers_General_Introduction</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA: GNSS Data Processing, Volume I</strong><br/>\nDetailed reference covering GNSS measurements, models and data processing.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/GNSS_Book/ESA_GNSS-Book_TM-23_Vol_I.pdf\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/GNSS_Book/ESA_GNSS-Book_TM-23_Vol_I.pdf</a></p>\n<p><strong>ESA Navipedia: GNSS Basic Observables</strong><br/>\nFurther explanation of pseudorange and other GNSS observables.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Basic_Observables\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://gssc.esa.int/navipedia/index.php/GNSS_Basic_Observables</a></p>\n<p><strong>European GNSS Service Centre: Galileo Open Service Reference Documents</strong><br/>\nCurrent Galileo Open Service interface and service reference material.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gsc-europa.eu/electronic-library/programme-reference-documents/galileo-in-force/open-service\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gsc-europa.eu/electronic-library/programme-reference-documents/galileo-in-force/open-service</a></p>\n<p><strong>GPS.gov: GPS Technical Documentation</strong><br/>\nOfficial GPS interface, performance and technical documentation.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://www.gps.gov/technical\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.gps.gov/technical</a></p>\n<p><strong>Royal Institute of Navigation: SALHUB PNT Training</strong><br/>\nExpert-led PNT curriculum covering introduction, systems engineering, receiver/data analysis, sensor integration, timing, test and resilience.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://rin.org.uk/event/SALHUBOnlinePNT\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://rin.org.uk/event/SALHUBOnlinePNT</a></p>\n<p><strong>GNSS Urban PNT Learning Lab (GitHub)</strong><br/>\nOptional practical learning resource. Useful later in the course for hands-on exploration of pseudorange positioning and challenging urban PNT scenarios.<br/>\n<a href=\"https://github.com/L1n-ker/gnss-urban-pnt-learning-lab\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">https://github.com/L1n-ker/gnss-urban-pnt-learning-lab</a></p>"}, {"id": "next-module", "title": "Next Module", "html": "<aside class=\"callout callout-note\"><p class=\"callout-title\">MODULE 03 — GNSS SIGNALS, ERRORS &amp; PERFORMANCE</p><div class=\"callout-body\"></div></aside>\n<p>Module 3 asks why a mathematically valid GNSS solution can still be wrong. It introduces signal frequencies, measurement quality, ionospheric and tropospheric delay, multipath, obstruction, receiver noise, satellite geometry and Dilution of Precision (DOP). It then connects those effects back to the Module 1 performance concepts of accuracy, availability, continuity and integrity.</p>"}], "practice": [{"n": 1, "prompt": "What two broad things must a receiver obtain from a useful GNSS satellite to contribute to positioning?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The satellite's colour and antenna size."}, {"id": "B", "text": "An estimate of the satellite's position and a range-related measurement."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The user's destination and map."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A cellular data connection."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "The receiver needs sufficiently accurate satellite location information and a measurement related to its apparent distance from that satellite."}, {"n": 2, "prompt": "Why is the basic code measurement called a pseudorange?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The satellites are not real."}, {"id": "B", "text": "It is an apparent range containing clock and other effects rather than the exact geometric distance."}, {"id": "C", "text": "It is measured in degrees rather than metres."}, {"id": "D", "text": "It only works in simulation."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Clock offsets and other effects mean the timing-derived measurement is not identical to true geometric range."}, {"n": 3, "prompt": "What can be carried in GNSS navigation data?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Satellite ephemeris."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Satellite clock correction information."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Satellite/service health information."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Almanac information."}, {"id": "E", "text": "The receiver's desired destination."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "Navigation data provides the information the receiver needs to interpret and use the satellite signal; the user's destination belongs to the application/navigation layer."}, {"n": 4, "prompt": "Which statement best distinguishes ephemeris and almanac?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Ephemeris is detailed orbit information for a satellite; almanac is coarser constellation-wide information."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Almanac is always more precise than ephemeris."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Ephemeris contains the user's map; almanac contains weather."}, {"id": "D", "text": "They are two names for the same data."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Ephemeris is precise and satellite-specific; almanac is coarser and describes the wider constellation."}, {"n": 5, "prompt": "Why is receiver clock bias solved as part of a conventional GNSS position solution?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Typical user receivers are not perfectly synchronised to GNSS system time."}, {"id": "B", "text": "GNSS satellites contain no clocks."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Radio signals travel at different speeds for each receiver."}, {"id": "D", "text": "The receiver must tell the satellite what time it is."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "The receiver clock is not assumed to be perfectly aligned with GNSS system time, so its offset is estimated with position."}, {"n": 6, "prompt": "Which activities occur inside a typical GNSS receiver processing chain?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Signal acquisition."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Signal tracking."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Navigation-message decoding."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Measurement formation."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Navigation solution estimation."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D", "E"], "rationale": "All are parts of the conceptual receiver chain from RF signal to PVT output."}, {"n": 7, "prompt": "Why can satellite geometry matter even if four satellites are available?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "Satellite colour changes the signal."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The spatial distribution of measurements affects how measurement errors map into the estimated solution."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Four satellites always give identical performance."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Geometry only matters to the control segment."}], "answer": ["B"], "rationale": "Measurement geometry affects the strength of the position solution. Module 3 develops this formally through DOP."}, {"n": 8, "prompt": "In normal GNSS positioning, which statement is correct?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The receiver must transmit its position to every satellite before receiving a fix."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Satellites normally calculate each user's position."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Satellites broadcast signals and navigation information; the receiver computes its own PVT solution."}, {"id": "D", "text": "A GNSS receiver cannot calculate time."}], "answer": ["C"], "rationale": "GNSS is normally broadcast/passive for the user receiver; the receiver performs the PVT computation."}, {"n": 9, "prompt": "Which four unknowns explain the familiar minimum of four measurements for a conventional 3D fix?", "multi": true, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "X coordinate."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Y coordinate."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Z coordinate."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Receiver clock bias."}, {"id": "E", "text": "Destination waypoint."}], "answer": ["A", "B", "C", "D"], "rationale": "Three spatial coordinates plus receiver clock bias give four primary unknowns in the basic model."}, {"n": 10, "prompt": "What is acquisition?", "multi": false, "options": [{"id": "A", "text": "The initial process of searching for and identifying a usable satellite signal in code/frequency space."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Purchasing a GNSS receiver."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Converting latitude into longitude."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Uploading the user's position to the control segment."}], "answer": ["A"], "rationale": "Acquisition is the initial search for a recognisable satellite signal and its code/frequency alignment."}]});
